                    {
                        "documentation": " Jumps to the next (or previous) placeholder in the current snippet, if possible.\n\n By default `<Tab>` is setup to jump if a snippet is active. The default mapping looks like:\n\n ```lua\n vim.keymap.set({ 'i', 's' }, '<Tab>', function()\n    if vim.snippet.active({ direction = 1 }) then\n      return '<Cmd>lua vim.snippet.jump(1)<CR>'\n    else\n      return '<Tab>'\n    end\n  end, { desc = '...', expr = true, silent = true })\n ```\n\n@*param* `direction` — Navigation direction. -1 for previous, 1 for next.\n\n```lua\ndirection:\n    | -1\n    | 1\n```",
